 draws and tells it. Out: no how the tools work inside, no dates beyond the order on the strip, no history of computing as a whole, no comparison of cultures' impacts, no job qualifications or named tools, no named products or companies. End-of-unit (day 31): match two new familiar machines to the problem each solves (E2-SOC-HU-15); place a new case card on the timeline and say what problem the tool solved for its people (E2-SOC-HI-14); spoken, one interest and one way computing is used in a job linked to it (E2-SOC-CE-16); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the input and output of unit 1 seen in machines that help people, and the algorithms and programs of units 2 and 3 seen as things people made. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 3, so it can follow the year-end check in week 26, and its five weeks fit before the year's end. Our own (the partial base's Year 3 has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses computing for a job; picture cards carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Khipu is Native Andean knowledge: told as the record-keeping of the Inca and other Andean peoples, not as a lost mystery, and as part of Andean life today where the sources say so; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Braille is told as a tool a blind person made for blind readers; no child is asked to act blind or wear a blindfold. No child is asked about a family member's job; a child's own offer is welcome. Pictures show many kinds of work, people and places, rural and town. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

