## 5. Cost, devices and rooms

Programming units run on a device per one or two students in a common browser; nothing is installed and no account is
made (bar criterion 7). Physical computing (a microcontroller board) is optional and always has a simulator or paper
stand-in; its cost is stated where used.

### Devices and rooms

Three things are kept apart. An unplugged day teaches an idea with cards, paper or movement; every unit has at least
one (kind: understanding; bar item 5). A device day is a day where a tool does a named job. A stand-in is how a device
day runs with fewer devices (C14). Every device day is written four ways (section fields 5 and 10): (1) one device per
pair, the default; (2) a few devices: three or four stations on a rotation, the other pairs at a paper station on the
same idea, every pair taking the keyboard role at least once; (3) one screen: the class runs the program together,
predicts and votes before each run, and a student at the device takes each step; (4) no device: the same idea on paper,
with trace tables, "be the computer", programs written on paper and checked by a partner. An id whose standard asks
students to use a computational tool, train a model, or use documentation and libraries is met only on a device (for
example E4-DAT-DC-09, E5-DAT-DC-09, E5-ALG-ML-02, MS-DAT-DC-23, MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-PRO-PD-13, HS-DAT-DC-21,
HS-DAT-DC-23, HS-ALG-ML-08, HS-PRO-PD-12, HS-PRO-PD-13, HS-SYS-HW-30). On the no-device path that day teaches the
idea and the course record marks the id "part met without a device"; the course never says the id was met.

### Generated-text tools

Evaluating generated output (MS-PRO-RD-18, HS-PRO-RD-18, HS-ALG-PS-05) is met on supplied material: Curio Class supplies
saved outputs and code samples, some right and some flawed, and students judge them for accuracy and use. No tool
asks a student to use a live generated-text tool, make an account or send anything off the device. For MS-ALG-PS-05,
which asks students to use one (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): no live tool in Curio
Class; the day is teacher-run on one screen with a tool the school has already approved and no student accounts, and
the course marks the id "part met" where a school has none.

### Our own editor

Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-1): build one small browser editor of our own (blocks and a
text view, with a step-through trace), a single file with no account, starting with grades 3 to 5, rather than pointing
teachers to a third-party tool. The text language for grades 6 to 12 is Python, run in this editor (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-1): the K to 8 partial base teaches Python in its middle years, so its units adapt directly, and it is
the most common first text language in high school.

The editor also keeps a small offline version history with branch, review and merge, so grade 12's team project
meets S1-SWD-PP-08 in full; until that exists, the standard is part met and the course page says so (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-2). It is an ask for the editor's own build list when the editor is specced.

### Blocks to text

Kindergarten to grade 5: block code with a text view of the same program. Grade 6: blocks and Python text side by
side. Grade 7: written Python for new programs, blocks as a stand-in. Grade 8 on: Python. Python is named as a subject
term on teacher pages and runs in our own editor in a common browser with nothing installed. The language is Cory's
(above); the grade steps are ours, untested.

### Privacy and safety

No student's name, photo, voice, location or family detail goes into a program, a dataset or a tool. Class data is about
things (weather, pets drawn from a list, shapes, snack types), tallied by hand or with counts, never about identifiable
children, health or home. Accounts, passwords and personal information are taught with a pretend page or a paper account;
no student makes an account. Online safety days use paper or an offline pretend site; any real web use is teacher-led on
one screen. Security days use toy systems built into the page; no scanning, no attack and no test of a real school or
home system, ever. A tool stores student work only on the device or in a file the student downloads or prints; it sends
nothing off the device.

### Real cases in society days

A real case is told by what happened, not by the company or product: "a company sold a face-matching system to police;
a 2018 study found it wrong more often for darker-skinned women" (example only, from memory, facts to be checked
before use). The teacher page gives the date and the source's title and publisher in a source line, which is a citation
and not a promotion. If a case cannot be understood without the name, choose another case or use a court or government
record's own description. A society day gives two views and one dated source for each.
Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): student and teacher copy may name government bodies and
courts but never companies, with sources only in the teacher source line.
# Computer science: approach (layer 3)

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-20 (Desk q-prework-cs-1). Written 2026-11-16, fixed 2026-11-19 after a cold read
(docs/reviews/prework-computer-science-read-2026-11-19.md); fields placed by number and sensitive ground gathered
2026-11-21 (docs/reviews/prework-computer-science-fixes-2026-11-20.md).** It fills the subject slots of the unit and
section plan (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) and leans on the shared approach (docs/APPROACH.md), the lens
(docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md) and the standard (STANDARD.md), which holds the rules this page points to:
ownership of MS and HS ids, depth, devices and rooms, privacy and safety, blocks to text, real cases, quoting. Ours,
untested.

## The kinds of day, in computer science's words

The `kind` column keeps the shared words; the goal says the activity.

| Shared kind | In computer science | What it looks like |
|---|---|---|
| Understanding | Unplugged or trace day | An idea taught with cards, paper or movement, or a given program traced and predicted before it is run |
| Fluency | Modify day | Students change a working program or algorithm in small, checked steps; short practice with the construct |
| Application | Make day, design day or society day | Students design and build their own program or artifact against criteria, or audit a real system or case with evidence |
| Check | Assessment day | The end-of-unit assessment (unit field 5, below) |
| Respond | Respond day | Fixes from the assessment, by error letter |

Order inside a unit: understanding before modify before make. A make day never comes before a trace or modify day on
the same construct.

## How a day opens (section field 6)

Every day opens in its first five minutes with one of three things. (a) A retrieval trace: a short program or algorithm
from an earlier unit, "what happens?", answered on paper, three minutes. (b) The wrong example from the last exit
ticket, if more than a third missed it. (c) For a new idea, one short concrete moment, unplugged, that the teaching then
names. Teaching first is the default for anything new: no blank editor, and no "try it" on a construct, before one worked
example has been shown and traced. In grades 6 to 12 a day may open with a predict task on a short given program (ten
minutes at most; ours, untested) when students have all they need to attempt it. The day row gives the opening and its
minutes.

## The form of daily evidence

- **Grades K to 2:** the answer is a pointing, sorting or ordering task, or said aloud to a partner and noted by the
  teacher by exception; a program is checked on the screen during the day.
- **Grades 3 to 12, trace or predict days:** an exit ticket with a short program or algorithm and the question "what
  happens?", answered in writing, with wrong answers by error letter (UNIT-PLAN rule 3).
- **Modify and make days:** the program itself, checked against two or three stated look-fors, plus one line from the
  student: what they tested and what they changed. A tool checks the look-fors it can (does the program do these three
  things on the tests?) and shows the student a "yes" or "not yet" for each; the teacher reads the open part for a
  rotating third of the class each session, so every student is read at least once in every three sessions (in K to
  5, which meets once a week, that is once in three weeks), and at the next session as well if the exit ticket shows
  a gap.
- **Unplugged days:** the paper artifact (the sorted cards, the drawn path, the encoded message) with one written
  question (K to 2: said or pointed).
- **Society days:** a short claim and two views, each with one dated source (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days").
- Student work is read on the device, or on a printout the student hands in.

## What computer science adds to the plan fields

Numbered as in docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md. Fields not named here (unit fields 1, 3, 4, 7 and 9; section fields 2 and 11)
are filled as UNIT-PLAN.md says, with nothing added for computer science.

**Unit plan.**
- Field 2: each standard's parts by verb (the id and our own words, STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the limits: the depth
  taught to and what is left out (STANDARD.md "Depth", from the owner row's notes); the practice the unit teaches,
  named by its code and our label (STANDARD.md section 3); what is
  carried through the unit: the anchor program, algorithm or dataset (its name, the day it is shown whole, the day it
  is withdrawn, the day students first build alone); the unit's unplugged days, counted.
- Field 5: read and trace items plus a short build or debug task (a society unit: a short audit or claim task). The
  task has look-fors and two sample programs or pieces, one that meets them and one that does not, and a paper version
  for a class with no device, marked as in STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms".
- Field 6: the unit's common bugs and notional-machine errors are lettered here with the other errors, one mistake
  per letter, each with what it shows.
- Field 8: each tool with its day, mark, job and verdict, and a link to the course's TOOL-ASKS.md; devices needed per
  pair; the supplies list, with the optional board's cost where one is used (STANDARD.md section 5); one line for the
  school's technical staff (a common browser, one file, nothing installed, no account, nothing sent off the device).
  **What a tool may and may not do:** a tool may step through a program line by line, run stated tests on a student
  program and show "yes" or "not yet" for each look-for, show blocks and text views of one program, and run a toy
  network, model or security system built into the page. Everything a tool may not do is set in STANDARD.md section
  5 ("Privacy and safety", "Generated-text tools") and under "The form of daily evidence" (the teacher reads the open
  part).
- Field 10: for a unit taken from the partial base, the base unit and its third-party check (STANDARD.md section 2,
  "Partial base for K to 8"); for each society case, its dated source (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days").

**Section plan.**
- Field 1: the notional-machine point (what the computer does with each line, in order) or, in a unit with no code, the
  adult-level background; the practice, its day and its task; in a section on sensitive ground, its teacher note (see
  "Sensitive ground").
- Field 3: each day's evidence in the form set under "The form of daily evidence".
- Field 4: each expected error as a wrong example beside a right one: a wrong program for code, a wrong statement for a
strand,grade,skill_id,standard,assumes,hands_on,model_in,model_out,words,cross_subject,notes
algorithms,K,k-follow-steps,EK-ALG-PS-01,,g1-split-task,,step-card,step; first; next; last; order; follow; plan; algorithm,,"Unplugged day. Children carry out everyday routines as ordered steps (washing hands, getting dressed, a snack line) and a made-up one (a robot dance), saying first, next, last. The word algorithm is taught in context: a set of steps in order that we follow is an algorithm. Introduces the step-card: one picture per step, laid left to right. Words are spoken and shown with pictures; nothing is read alone. This is the base row for every algorithm row after it."
algorithms,K,k-people-steps,EK-ALG-IM-03; EK-SOC-HU-15,,g1-change-steps; g1-people-or-machine,step-card,step-card,make; design; invent; people; helper; rule,,"Unplugged day. A person made each routine and each game rule, so a person can change it: the class changes one step of a working step-card set and checks what happens. With the teacher, children describe in simple words how a person makes an algorithm: choose a task, notice its smaller parts, put the steps in order to reach the goal (breaking a task into parts on one's own is grade 1's g1-split-task). Then children make a step-card set for a game they know, and a classmate follows it and says whether it worked. Tells the class that people design the tools around them (a spoon, a pencil, a game, a computer) and the steps they run: a person thought of a game's rules, drew its characters and programmed it. Part of this row is met by talk and drawing; no device needed."
programming,K,k-sequence-commands,EK-PRO-PD-04,,g1-code-sequence,step-card,step-card; block-program,command; program; forward; turn; left; right; blocks,math:mk-shapes-positions,"Unplugged first: a child moves a partner or a toy along a floor grid using arrow cards, then lays the cards in order as a program. Device day: the same short path built from large picture blocks in a block language, one block per step, run and watched. Introduces the block-program. A worked path is shown and traced before any child builds alone. Device versions follow STANDARD.md (one device per pair, stations, one screen, or paper arrow cards)."
programming,K,k-describe-sequence,EK-PRO-RD-06,,g1-explain-events,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,tell; describe; does; happens; because,,"Given a short block program or an arrow-card line, children say aloud what it makes the character do, step by step, before it runs, then check by running or by walking it. Teacher notes the answer by exception. Reading is by picture only. Unplugged day with a device check; the paper path needs no screen."
programming,K,k-wrong-step,EK-PRO-TR-07,,g1-fix-sequence,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,bug; fix; wrong; broken; try again; test,,"A given program that does not reach the goal (the toy stops short, turns the wrong way). Children point to the step where it first goes wrong and say what to change. Bug is taught with both meanings, an insect and a mistake in a program. Errors are normal and shared. Unplugged with arrow cards, or device day with a one-block fix."
programming,K,k-symbols,EK-PRO-VD-05,,g1-changing-things,,,sign; symbol; gesture; thumbs up; traffic light; means; choose,,"Unplugged day. Everyday signs and gestures carry information that people use to choose: a red light, a thumbs up, a wave, an arrow, a clock hand. Children read pictured signs and gesture cards and say what each tells them to do. This is the first idea of a value that stands for something; no variable is taught. Spoken and pictured words only."
algorithms,K,k-sort-attributes,EK-ALG-ML-02,,g1-patterns-ai,,attribute-cards,group; same; different; color; shape; size; pattern; sort,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. Children sort real objects and picture cards by one attribute (color, size, shape, wet or dry), find what the group has in common and then guess which group a new card belongs to. This is how machines that sort things first work, taught without the machine: notice what is the same, then decide. Introduces the attribute-cards. No device needed."
data,K,k-ask-and-count,EK-DAT-DI-09; EK-DAT-DC-08,,g1-two-kinds-data; g1-data-questions,,picture-graph,question; count; more; fewer; most; graph; answer,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. The class asks a question about its own room (which game, which weather, which class-library book) and collects the answers by placing a block or sticker per child on a floor or wall picture graph, then reads it to answer. The teacher poses the first question; children pose the next one from a picture prompt. Introduces the picture-graph. Class data is about things, tallied by hand; no child is named. Used again with every later data row."
data,K,k-data-decide,EK-DAT-IM-10,,g1-data-questions,picture-graph,picture-graph,choose; decide; because; plan; vote,math:mk-sort-count,"Unplugged day. The class uses its own picture graph to make a real choice (which game to play, which book to read) and says what the graph showed. Children name one other everyday decision that people make from counts (what to pack when it rains, what to cook). Said aloud and pointed to; teacher notes by exception."
systems,K,k-tools-jobs,EK-SYS-HW-11,,g1-hardware-parts,,,tool; job; use; computer; tablet; button; screen; touch,,"Unplugged and device day. Children match tools to jobs for different people (a spoon and a fork, a magnifier, a pencil grip, a screen reader, a keyboard) and name the parts of a device they touch or look at. Asks who a tool works for and who it does not, the first human-centered idea. Device version: a few devices at stations, or one screen with a child at the device for each step."
systems,K,k-device-manners,EK-SYS-IM-13,,g1-responsible-use,,,careful; share; turn; ask; kind; safe; rules,,"Unplugged and device day. The class makes simple rules for using shared devices and tools (take turns, ask before using, handle with two hands, stop when told) and acts out a right way and a wrong way. Children say why each rule helps people. Said aloud; no reading."
systems,K,k-public-private,EK-SYS-SE-12,,g1-keep-safe,,,public; private; share; secret; name; address; password,,"Unplugged day. Children sort picture cards into what is fine to tell anyone (a favorite color) and what only family or a trusted adult should know (a home address, a password). No real personal detail is used or asked for. Introduces the idea that private things need protecting, which later rows turn into accounts and passwords."
society,K,k-devices-places,EK-SOC-CE-16,,g1-computing-around,,,home; school; work; use; device; helper; job,,"Unplugged day. Children point out and name devices in pictures of a home, a school and a workplace (a store checkout, a clinic, a farm), and say who uses each and for what. No named products. Builds the sense that computing is part of many jobs."
society,K,k-then-now,EK-SOC-HI-14,,g1-then-and-now,,timeline,before; after; then; now; change; old; new; long ago,,"Unplugged day. Paired pictures of an older and a newer version of a computing device used at school or at home (a corded phone with push buttons and a flat touch-screen phone, a deep box-shaped screen and a flat screen, a large desk computer and a tablet) are put in order on a two-card strip, older on the left. Children say what looks different (smaller, flatter, no cord, a touch screen). Introduces the timeline as a short strip of then and now. No dates, inventors or how the parts work inside; comparing how an everyday activity was done before and after a technology arrived is grade 1's g1-then-and-now. No child is asked about the devices at home; a child's own offer is welcome."
algorithms,1,g1-split-task,E1-ALG-PS-01,k-follow-steps,g2-algorithm-loops,step-card,step-card,break down; part; whole; step; list; task; goal,,"Unplugged day. A bigger task (make a sandwich, tidy the room, build a block tower) is broken into small parts and each part into steps on cards. Children check by having a partner carry the cards out. Builds on the K step-card by adding the idea of parts of a task. The cards become the plan for the next programming row. Device version: not required; the idea is met unplugged."
algorithms,1,g1-change-steps,E1-ALG-IM-03,k-people-steps,g2-algorithm-peers,step-card,step-card,change; different; result; swap; same; instead,,"Unplugged day. A working step-card plan is changed (swap two steps, drop one, add one) and children predict what comes out differently, then check by carrying it out. Explains in a sentence that a change to the steps leads to a different result. Builds on K: a person made the plan, so a person can change it."
algorithms,1,g1-patterns-ai,E1-ALG-ML-02,k-sort-attributes,g2-ai-training-data,attribute-cards,attribute-cards,pattern; example; guess; sort; machine; learn; picture; data,,"Unplugged day. Children sort picture cards, then play a guessing game where a partner who has seen a few examples guesses which group a new card is in by the pattern. Names the idea that some computer systems find patterns in many examples to make a decision or make something new. Said simply, with no claim that a machine thinks. The attribute-cards from K carry on."
data,1,g1-two-kinds-data,E1-DAT-DC-08,k-ask-and-count,g2-data-types,picture-graph,picture-graph,number; count; word; describe; collect; ask; watch; record,math:m1-data-three-categories,"Unplugged day. The class collects both counts (how many chairs, how many sunny days) and descriptions (the color of a leaf, how a crayon feels) using asking, watching and measuring, and records each on a picture graph or a labelled card. Uses more than one way to collect, then answers a question with the mix. Number and word data are named as types in grade 2."
data,1,g1-data-questions,E1-DAT-DI-09; E1-DAT-IM-10,k-ask-and-count; k-data-decide,g2-data-questions,picture-graph,picture-graph,question; data; ask; answer; need; community; family,math:m1-data-three-categories,"Unplugged day. Children sort questions into those that counting can answer (how many pets does the class have) and those that need something else (which pet is best). Then they look at questions people in the school or town might ask to meet a need (when does the bus come, which garden needs water). Data work stays on class things, never on named children. They also see how changing one word changes what a question asks and who it fits (do you have a pet, do you look after a pet)."
programming,1,g1-code-sequence,E1-PRO-PD-04,k-sequence-commands,g2-code-loops,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,code; event; start; when; sequence; character,,"Device day, with an unplugged day first. Children turn a step-card plan from g1-split-task into blocks, including one event block (when this is tapped, start), and run it. The worked example is shown and traced before anyone builds alone. Paper stand-in: arrow cards with a start card. A text view of the same program is shown to the teacher only."
programming,1,g1-explain-events,E1-PRO-RD-06,k-describe-sequence,g2-explain-build,block-program,block-program,event; tap; press; what happens; because; order,,"Children read a short given block program that starts on an event and say what it does and why, before running it. Predict, then run, then compare. The K idea that order matters returns with an event: nothing happens until the event. Spoken or pointed answers; no reading beyond icons."
programming,1,g1-fix-sequence,E1-PRO-TR-07,k-wrong-step,g2-debug-loops,block-program; step-card,block-program; step-card,debug; bug; find; fix; test; again; first wrong,,"A given block program with one wrong step or one out of order. Children trace it, find the first step that is wrong, change one thing, and test again. This is the debugging method in four words: predict, run, find, fix. Unplugged with arrow cards on a floor grid, or device day."
programming,1,g1-changing-things,E1-PRO-VD-05,k-symbols,g2-name-and-label,,,change; stay the same; day; weather; height; age; count; time,,"Unplugged day. Children sort pictured things by whether the value changes over time (the weather, a plant's height, the date, their age) or stays the same (a birthday, a name). This is a first look at a changing value without a program. Examples use plants, weather and clocks, not named people."
society,1,g1-computing-around,E1-SOC-CE-16,k-devices-places,g2-interests-careers,,,community; library; shop; clinic; use; digital; school,,"Unplugged day. A picture tour of home, school and town: who uses a computer or a digital device there, and for what (checking out a book, paying, a map, a doorbell). Children draw one place and one use. No named products or companies."
society,1,g1-then-and-now,E1-SOC-HI-14,k-then-now,g2-people-solve,timeline,timeline,before; after; compare; change; activity; invention; long ago,,"Unplugged day. Take one everyday activity (talking to a faraway friend, finding a place, listening to music) and lay out how it was done before and after a technology arrived on the two-card timeline. Children say what got easier, faster or different. Facts about dates are from memory and are checked by the teacher before use."
society,1,g1-people-or-machine,E1-SOC-HU-15,k-people-steps,g2-people-solve,,,good at; people; computer; fast; tired; creative; check; kind,,"Unplugged day. Sort a set of activities into what people do well (comfort a friend, invent a game, notice a mood) and what a computer does well (add a long list fast, repeat the same step without tiring, store a lot). Some activities go in both. No claim that computers feel or think."
systems,1,g1-hardware-parts,E1-SYS-HW-11,k-tools-jobs,g2-io-parts,,,keyboard; mouse; screen; speaker; camera; hardware; part; job,,"Unplugged and device day. Children name the basic parts of a device (screen, keyboard, mouse or touchpad, speaker, camera, microphone) with the right words and say each part's purpose. Match part to job on a pictured device and on the real one. One device per pair, or one on the class screen."
systems,1,g1-responsible-use,E1-SYS-IM-13,k-device-manners,g2-benefits-harms,,,responsible; careful; share; ask; rules; kind; time,,"Unplugged day. Children say what a responsible user does with shared devices and tools: asks first, takes turns, keeps things clean and safe, tells an adult when something seems wrong. Acted out with a short story card, no real accounts. Builds on the K rules."
systems,1,g1-keep-safe,E1-SYS-SE-12,k-public-private,g2-online-consequences,,,password; account; lock; safe; secret; share; adult,,"Unplugged day. Introduces a password as a private key to an account and a lock as a picture. Children practise a pretend login on paper, sort what to share (a drawing) and what to keep private (a password), and say what to do if someone asks for it. No real account is made by any child."
algorithms,2,g2-algorithm-loops,E2-ALG-PS-01,g1-split-task,g3-algorithm-combo,step-card,step-card,repeat; loop; again; times; event; when; algorithm,math:m2-arrays-equal-addends,"Unplugged day. Children write an algorithm that has an ordered sequence, one event (when the bell rings) and a repeat (clap three times) for a dance, a drawing or a game, then a partner follows it. The repeat shortens the step-cards: a card says repeat 3 and holds the steps that repeat. Builds on splitting a task into parts. Device version: not required."
algorithms,2,g2-algorithm-peers,E2-ALG-IM-03,g1-change-steps,g3-algorithm-effects,step-card,step-card,fair; peer; different; people; needs; rule; game,,"Unplugged day. A game's rules are an algorithm. Children play a game as written and notice who it works well for and who it leaves out (the rule that needs a fast runner), then say whether everyone had a fair chance and whether anyone might never get a turn. The same rule can work well for one child and badly for another. Judging fairness is the skill; designing a fairer rule is not asked in grade 2."
algorithms,2,g2-ai-training-data,E2-ALG-ML-02,g1-patterns-ai,g3-ml-new-data,attribute-cards,training-set,train; training; example; data; label; model; guess; wrong,,"Unplugged day. A class sorter learns from labelled example cards: students act as the machine, look at the labelled examples (the training set) and then guess new cards. Children see that a guess is only as good as the examples it learned from, for instance when every example of a bird has wings spread. Introduces the training-set. A device sorting tool may be shown on one screen."
programming,2,g2-code-loops,E2-PRO-PD-04,g1-code-sequence,g3-make-improve,step-card; block-program,step-card; block-program,repeat; loop; event; sequence; code; command; run,math:m2-arrays-equal-addends,"Device day, with an unplugged day first. Children turn the algorithm from g2-algorithm-loops into blocks with a sequence, an event and a repeat block. Worked example first and traced, then a version with blanks, then a modify, then a build. Paper stand-in: step-cards with a repeat card, acted out by a partner."
programming,2,g2-explain-build,E2-PRO-RD-06,g1-explain-events,g3-read-code,block-program,block-program,plan; build; test; change; steps; because; program,ela:e2-tell-and-record,"Children say the steps they took to make a program: what the plan was, what they built first, what they tested, what they changed. Uses the sentence frame First I ___, then I ___. A finished program is shown and explained in order. Spoken or drawn, with the teacher noting by exception. Each child also says which part was their own idea and which part a classmate or the teacher helped with."
programming,2,g2-debug-loops,E2-PRO-TR-07,g1-fix-sequence,g3-debug-combo,block-program,block-program,debug; loop; too many; not enough; fix; test; first wrong,,"A given program with a bug in a repeat (it repeats too many times, an event does not start) or a sequence. Children predict, run, find the first place it goes wrong, change one thing and test. Devices or arrow cards on a floor grid. Tracing is by pointing and counting with fingers."
programming,2,g2-name-and-label,E2-PRO-VD-05,g1-changing-things,g3-variable-trace,,named-box,name; label; value; constant; changes; box; score; time,,"Unplugged day. Children label things that hold information with a name and say whether the value stays the same or changes: the score, the date, a pet's name, the time. A named box, a cup with a label, holds one value; a card shows what is inside. This is the first named-box. No variable is used in code yet; a score on a game screen is shown and labelled."
data,2,g2-data-types,E2-DAT-DC-08,g1-two-kinds-data,g3-check-data; g3-attributes,picture-graph,picture-graph; data-table,number; word; count; describe; table; row; column; collect,math:m2-picture-bar-graphs,"Unplugged day. Compare number data (how tall) and word data (what color) in how each is collected (measure, ask, look) and what each tells. Both go into a two-column paper data-table. Introduces the data-table as rows and columns on paper. The picture-graph and the table are put side by side."
data,2,g2-data-questions,E2-DAT-DI-09; E2-DAT-IM-10,g1-data-questions,g3-attributes; g3-data-groups,picture-graph; data-table,data-table,ask; question; fair; biased; sample; leave out; guess; collect,math:m2-picture-bar-graphs,"Unplugged day. Children turn a vague question into one data can answer (what do kids like becomes which snack does our class pick most), set aside a question that data cannot answer, then compare ways to collect (ask only the front row, ask everyone, ask by hand raise versus in secret) and say which could give a wrong or unfair answer and why. Uses class things only. A simple bias is named as leaving some people out. They say how a one-sided way of asking could lead to an unfair choice for the class, such as a game picked by the front row only."
society,2,g2-interests-careers,E2-SOC-CE-16,g1-computing-around,g3-computing-at-work,,,career; job; interest; hobby; build; create; help; computer,,"Unplugged day. Children connect something they like (animals, art, sports, music, cooking) to ways computing is used in jobs that involve it (a vet clinic record, a sound studio, a sports score system). Drawn and told; no company or product names."
society,2,g2-people-solve,E2-SOC-HI-14; E2-SOC-HU-15,g1-then-and-now; g1-people-or-machine,g3-why-build,timeline,timeline,culture; invent; long ago; problem; solve; tool; express,,"Unplugged day. Case cards show people in different places and times who made a tool for counting, writing, mapping or making music to solve a problem or share an idea (the abacus; knotted cords for records; from memory, the teacher checks the sources). Children place the tools on the timeline and say what problem each solved. Shows that many people everywhere have designed computing tools."
systems,2,g2-io-parts,E2-SYS-HW-11,g1-hardware-parts,g3-software; g3-reach-internet,,system-diagram,input; output; processing; keyboard; screen; speaker; work together,,"Unplugged and device day. Introduces the input-to-output picture, the system-diagram: an input part takes in, the computer parts do something, an output part shows or sounds. Children sort parts as input or output (a mouse is input, a speaker is output) and trace one action from a touch to a change on the screen."
systems,2,g2-benefits-harms,E2-SYS-IM-13,g1-responsible-use,g3-benefits-risks,,benefit-risk-chart,good; harm; help; hurt; too much; screen time; choose,,"Unplugged day. A two-column benefit-risk-chart, one side for how a tool helps and one for how it can hurt, for one tool a child uses (a video call, a game, a drawing tool). Children add one thing each way and say what a person could do about the harm. Introduces the benefit-risk-chart."
systems,2,g2-online-consequences,E2-SYS-SE-12,g1-keep-safe,g3-share-online,benefit-risk-chart,benefit-risk-chart,online; post; share; real; forever; kind; hurtful; pretend,,"Unplugged day. With a pretend page on paper, children see that what someone posts or sends is seen by real people and can be kept and passed on. Sort kind and unkind messages and say what happens next to the sender and the reader. No real account or message is made."
algorithms,3,g3-algorithm-combo,E3-ALG-PS-01,g2-algorithm-loops,g4-written-algorithm,step-card,step-card,if; else; choose; decide; condition; algorithm; combine; repeat,,"Unplugged day. Children write an algorithm that combines sequence, an event, a repeat and a choice (if it is raining, take the umbrella; else take a hat) for a game, a route or an act. Selection is introduced here with a yes or no question and two paths. A partner follows the written steps exactly, which shows where a step is missing. In-grade order for grade 3: g3-make-improve (block-and-text-view) before g3-read-code, then g3-variable-trace (trace-table), then g3-debug-combo."
algorithms,3,g3-algorithm-effects,E3-ALG-IM-03,g2-algorithm-peers,g4-algorithm-groups,step-card; benefit-risk-chart,step-card; benefit-risk-chart,fair; effect; compare; different; people; needs; outcome,,"Unplugged day. Two ways of doing the same job (first come first served, or a time slot for each) are carried out on the class and compared by their effect on people with different needs (a student who needs more time, a student who is new). Children use a benefit-risk-chart per way. Builds on g2-algorithm-peers."
algorithms,3,g3-ml-new-data,E3-ALG-ML-02,g2-ai-training-data,g4-ml-learning,training-set,training-set,training set; add; new; change; better; model; same,,"Unplugged day. A card sorter built from a small training-set is tested and then given more labeled examples and tested again. Children compare what it gets right before and after and say why it changed: adding examples of the kind it saw least helped. Run as a class game or, on one screen, with a simple sorting tool."
data,3,g3-check-data,E3-DAT-DC-09,g2-data-types,g4-table-tool,data-table,data-table,accurate; complete; missing; wrong; check; fix; number; word,math:m3-scaled-graphs,"Unplugged day. A paper data-table with a few wrong entries and gaps (a missing cell, a height of 400 inches) is given. Children check it for accuracy and completeness, say which entries to ask about again and which to leave out. Number and word data are both checked. Device version: none required."

